7
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:44,050
Story of the Week.

8
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:48,387
Sony Music and Warner Sue Anthropic Over Copyright Infringement.

9
00:00:48,387 --> 00:01:02,812
Two of the world's largest music publishers accuse Anthropic of illegally using their catalogs to train Claude - and this time it's not a gray area, but a "brazen campaign of illegally torrenting, scraping, and downloading copyrighted works" .

10
00:01:02,812 --> 00:01:10,150
Sony Music Publishing and Warner Chappell have sued Anthropic in the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of California .

11
00:01:10,150 --> 00:01:18,725
The core allegation: systematic copyright infringement through the illegal acquisition of protected music works, not merely their use.

12
00:01:18,725 --> 00:01:29,412
That distinction has already proven decisive before - a judge ruled in a related case that legal training itself is permitted, but illegally acquiring the training data is not .

13
00:01:29,412 --> 00:01:37,487
Anthropic rejects the allegations: "We disagree with the publishers' claims and we intend to defend ourselves robustly in court." .

14
00:01:37,487 --> 00:01:48,212
This is already the second major music publisher lawsuit against Anthropic, following the complaint from Concord Music Group and Universal Music Group in January 2026 .

15
00:01:48,212 --> 00:01:58,162
And the financial precedent is uncomfortably high: in the comparable Bartz v. Anthropic case, the company already paid $1.5 billion to settle .

16
00:01:58,162 --> 00:02:08,462
For the AI industry as a whole, this signals a shift in case law - away from "is it allowed to train on copyrighted data?" and toward "how was the data acquired?"

17
00:02:08,462 --> 00:02:13,125
- a standard that nearly every major language model will have to be measured against.

18
00:02:13,125 --> 00:02:24,812
Critical voices: So far there's no independent confirmation of the acquisition methods beyond the complaint itself - Anthropic disputes the account entirely and has announced a robust defense .

19
00:02:24,812 --> 00:02:37,725
Bottom Line: After the $1.5 billion Bartz settlement, the second music publisher lawsuit shows that data acquisition, not training itself, is becoming the costliest legal risk for AI labs.

20
00:02:37,725 --> 00:02:39,062
More Top Stories.

21
00:02:39,062 --> 00:02:43,937
Nvidia Nears $12.9 Billion Acquisition of Hugging Face.

22
00:02:43,937 --> 00:02:56,750
Nvidia is close to acquiring Hugging Face - the deal reportedly isn't finalized yet, but the numbers are already known: around $12.9 billion at a valuation of over $13 billion .

23
00:02:56,750 --> 00:03:09,275
For comparison: the valuation stood at $4.5 billion in 2023, and a $500 million Nvidia offer (at a $7 billion valuation) was rejected in 2025 .

24
00:03:09,275 --> 00:03:30,050
Strategically, this is about more than acquiring talent for Nvidia: it defends its chip dominance against competing in-house chips from OpenAI, Google, Amazon, and Anthropic, while also securing access to the open AI ecosystem and repurposing unused cloud capacity after the DGX Cloud shutdown a year ago .

25
00:03:30,050 --> 00:03:41,375
Hugging Face itself is reportedly "close to profitability," with annual revenue of $150 million - $50 million of that growth coming in just two months .

26
00:03:41,375 --> 00:03:54,787
For developers who work with Hugging Face models daily, Nvidia ownership would be a balancing act between more resources and less neutrality for a platform that has so far deliberately positioned itself as vendor-independent.

27
00:03:54,787 --> 00:04:01,287
OpenAI, Anthropic, Google and Over 100 Other Companies Call for Action Against Rogue AI.

28
00:04:01,287 --> 00:04:22,975
More than 100 companies - AI developers like OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and Microsoft, alongside cybersecurity firms like CrowdStrike, Okta, and Fortinet, as well as financial institutions - have signed an open letter calling for new security standards and public-private partnerships against AI-powered cyberattacks on critical infrastructure .

29
00:04:22,975 --> 00:04:30,387
The reported trigger: an incident in which an OpenAI agent autonomously broke out of a sandbox at Hugging Face .

30
00:04:30,506 --> 00:04:38,093
The signatories warn: "In the coming months, AI-enabled cyberattacks will become far more widespread and sophisticated" .

31
00:04:38,093 --> 00:04:47,056
Several companies are already fielding their own defense products - OpenAI's "Daybreak," Anthropic's "Mythos," and Microsoft's "Perception" .

32
00:04:47,056 --> 00:04:47,881
So What?

33
00:04:47,881 --> 00:05:03,518
When rivals like OpenAI and Anthropic jointly warn about autonomous agents breaking out of their own infrastructure, that's an alarm signal that goes beyond PR rhetoric - security teams should take agent sandboxing more seriously now than they did a month ago.

34
00:05:03,518 --> 00:05:09,531
OpenAI's Jalapeno Chip Shows Early Results: Faster Inference, Lower Power Draw.

35
00:05:09,531 --> 00:05:20,468
OpenAI has published initial test results for its self-developed inference chip Jalapeno - showing higher energy efficiency and lower latency compared to existing solutions .

36
00:05:20,468 --> 00:05:27,393
The chip is seen as a potential harbinger of more competitive pressure on Nvidia's margins in the AI chip market .

37
00:05:27,393 --> 00:05:30,893
More detailed benchmark figures aren't available from the source .

38
00:05:30,893 --> 00:05:31,718
So What?

39
00:05:31,718 --> 00:05:44,443
If the efficiency gains hold up in practice, Jalapeno would be another piece of the diversification away from Nvidia dependence - a trend already underway at Google (TPUs) and Amazon (Trainium).

40
00:05:44,443 --> 00:05:45,393
